I'm joined today by vivica Novak who is  an investigative editor for McClatchy  cheese Washington bureau she's been a  journalist in Washington DC for more  than 20 years vivica it's so great to  talk to you you have been following what  appears to be a sort of funding funnel  from Russia through the NRA and to  Donald Trump how was this even something  that initially got on your radar well we  had been hearing actually for quite some  time that this may have been happening  and it took us months and months to get  a second source on it but we were  eventually able to get a second source  saying that Robert Muller's  investigation is looking into whether  Russian money was funneled through the  NRA to help drama is there anything that  separates the legality or illegality of  Russian money to trump through the NRA  versus all the other hypothetical  avenues that have been explored or  mentioned so far well you're right there  are a number of avenues one thing that  is pretty clear is that it is illegal to  use foreign money to influence a US  election so that if the NRA was using  Russian money in its expenditures to  help Trump which were considerable in  the 2016 election then that would pretty  clearly be illegal can you talk a little  bit about I mean I'm interested in both  the story itself but also how you work a  story like this so what is the sort of  first step in starting to source a story  like this what is the sort of work on  the ground that is done well we've been  working at the Trump Russia story since  January or before and we were the first  u.s. outlet to publish a story saying  that the connection was under  investigation Russian interference in  the election was under investigation and  that they were looking at the Trump  campaign and that eventually became  Robert Muller's investigation when he  was named special counsel later last  year  so we have been working a number of  sources to try to figure out what  exactly happened and what Muller's team  is looking at since then and so this is  a process of building you build sources  who may lead you to other sources and  you circle back to your sources and you  try to get any documentary evidence you  can it's it's a laborious time-consuming  process and McClatchy is not being the  Washington Post or the New York Times we  sometimes don't get the same kind of  leaks from on high that some of the more  prominent news organizations do but I  have to hand it to our reporters we've  got a great team with a lot of  experience and they've been dogged in  this are there long periods of time in  an investigation like this we're just  sort of nothing really happens yes you  just work on other stories during that  time or is there a lot of sort of  waiting around for sources in some sense  well I think from the reporters  standpoint there's a lot of fretting  when your sources aren't getting back to  you you haven't learned anything lately  you know what's happening so there is a  lot of sort of spreading time I call it  and I think reporters in general it's  not like you're sitting still you are  working other angles of the story you're  trying to meet people you're trying to  set up coffees you're doing this or that  in the usual reporting process from an  editor standpoint you're kind of sitting  there thinking hmm when is this going to  happen is it going to happen so there's  probably more downtime for someone like  me in an editor position than there is  for the reporters I want to start  digging into sort of the meat of the  story in a second but one more sort of  question in terms of process I it's  become very very sort of popular for  people who hear a story that they don't  necessarily like the implications of to  say hey you know it it lists anonymous  sources it's not naming the people and  it there's this sort of reflexive  tendency increasingly to  right off any story that doesn't name  sources but this misses something that's  really important which is that when you  for example say we have two sources  familiar with the matter that's very  different than saying we have an  anonymous source or we have three  sources who work within X or Y  Department the the wording of how  sources are named actually carries a lot  of weight in these types of stories can  you talk about that a little bit and the  meaning of that well it does it's always  a tricky balancing act because you want  to protect your source who you know may  be fearful of losing their job or may be  disclosing something that is  confidential or sometimes even  classified so you want to protect that  source and at the same time you want to  give the reader a little bit of  information to indicate that you're not  just you know pulling this from someone  on the street so we do try to do that  but it's um it's tricky sometimes let's  talk now about sort of the meat and the  funneling of this funding we know that  the NRA spent a lot of money in the 2016  election including 30 million dollars in  support of Donald Trump that's a lot  more than they spent in 2012 backing  Mitt Romney for example so talk to us a  little bit about the process of your  investigation and sort of the details of  how this all what was arguably put  together well yes we knew that the NRA  spent three times as much money in 2016  backing Trump as as it did in 2012  backing Mitt Romney we also knew that  there was this Russian named tortion  Alexander tortion who was a very  enthusiastic NRA supporter and came over  for some of the annual conventions the  NRA has and I mean in the process of  building the information we  learned that you know Donald Trump jr.  had been to one of the NRS conventions  and had spoken with this torsion fellow  that torsion had hosted a big delegation  of NRA officials over in Russia and it  started up a sort of Russian offshoot in  a way of the NRA called the right to  bear arms so there were there were a  number of pieces of information that we  began accumulating and it was kind of  you know it was kind of a matter of  trying to figure out whether this could  have happened and you know again we  don't know whether it happened but we do  know that it is being investigated by  the Malheur team people might hear us  talking about this and they might say  not unreasonably that it should be as  simple as just looking at the list of  the NRA s donors but as your  investigation turned up it's not quite  so simple is it well for one thing you  can't look at a list of the NRA donors  dnr a does not make its members or  donors public and the group the offshoot  that it uses to spend money in the  elections is a 501 C 4 a nonprofit that  doesn't have to disclose its donors so  yeah that is that is not that would be  great but it is not exactly how you can  get any information bigger picture what  do you see is the most likely sort of  eventual conclusion of everything that's  going on with Trump and Russia  I mean I'm hearing from people that run  the full gamut you know from impeachment  indictment all the way to this is going  to evaporate with nothing conclusive and  really no major charges from Robert  Muller you've been following this for a  long time do you have any opinion as to  what's the most likely outcome here you  know I have been following it for a long  time as has my team and I would say I  don't know what the outcome will be  you know you have a couple of people  under indictment  maybe going to trial in August or  September of this year there may be more  people cooperating that we don't know  about I think there probably are what's  it all going to boil down to is it going  to boil down to accusations about money  laundering against Trump that would have  happened probably before the election is  it going to boil down to some kind of  collusion is an illegal term but  cooperation or conspiring with Russia to  interfere in the elections is it going  to come down to obstruction of justice  you know that there are some data points  there that are you know kind of  convincing but we don't know ultimately  we know that Robert Muller has a number  of strands of his investigation he's  following we don't know which ones if  any are going to eventually result in  you know something that would be  critical of or charging the President or  if he would produce a report and that  would be sent to Congress and  impeachment proceedings may or may not  start it's ultimately still just very up  in the air what to sort of approach the  question from a slightly different angle  at this point would you be surprised if  it fizzled basically from this point  forward and nothing more really came of  it would that be a surprising outcome to  you I'd be surprised if it fizzled and  nothing more came of it yeah I would be  but but that doesn't necessarily mean  that you know Donald Trump will be the  subject of a very critical report it  could be it could be something involving  Jared Kushner or there there could be  other people who will be either charged  or the subject of a very critical report  in the end we've been speaking with  vivica Novak she is an investigative  editor for McClatchy Washington Bureau  which has been doing some excellent  excellent work thank you so much for  talking to us about this thank you it's  good to be with you
 

